---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StepDiagram from '../components/StepDiagram.astro';
import TrustStrip from '../components/TrustStrip.astro';
import Inspector from '../components/Inspector.astro';
import { evaluation, model } from '../lib/data';
import { thousands } from '../lib/format';

const order = ['add', 'sub', 'a+2b', 'a<<1', 'xor', 'and', 'max', 'transducer', 'a+b+1', '3a', 'a-2b', 'nand', 'min', 'a*b'];
const heldout = new Set(model.families.heldout);
const notes: Record<string, string> = {
  min: `held out · ${Math.round((evaluation.results.heldout.min?.exact ?? 0) * 100)}% exact — never commits to one direction`,
  max: 'carries a “which is bigger” decision from the top bit down',
  xor: 'no carry at all: either direction works',
};
const trustRows = order.map((name) => ({
  name: heldout.has(name) ? `${name} *` : name,
  value: model.pass_trust[name] ?? 0,
  note: notes[name],
}));
---

<Layout title="First model" description="The first learned model: a recurrent loop over bit positions with a lookup over the examples.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Changelog · first model</p>
  <h1>The first model: bit by bit</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Kept for the record. This model read every example one bit position at a time — lowest bit first in one pass,
    highest first in another. It solved every relation it trained on, but could not handle multiplication or decide
    which direction to read for <code>min</code>; the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/model/`}>current
    model</a> replaced it. Its training and benchmark pages:
    <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/learning/`}>how it learned</a> ·
    <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/benchmarks/`}>benchmarks</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first learned model (447K parameters, superseded) walked through the bit positions, remembered a little about the bits so far, and at each bit
    copied the answer from the examples in the same situation as the question. It is still the easiest to look inside,
    so the replay below uses it.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-2 ideas">
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">1 · Loop over bits</p>
      <p>
        A small recurrent network (GRU) reads <code>(a<sub>k</sub>, b<sub>k</sub>, c<sub>k</sub>)</code> one position at
        a time, for <strong>every</strong> example and for the question. The same weights run at every position.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">2 · Remember what matters</p>
      <p>
        Its memory has to hold just enough about the bits already read — a carry for <code>add</code>, a borrow for
        <code>sub</code>, “a is already bigger” for <code>max</code>. Nobody tells it which; training does.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">3 · Look up similar situations</p>
      <p>
        At bit <code>k</code>, the question attends over the 999 examples. Examples whose current bits and memory match
        get the attention; their known <code>c<sub>k</sub></code> becomes the vote. The question's own guess feeds back
        into its memory for the next bit.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">4 · Check both directions</p>
      <p>
        One pass reads from the lowest bit, one from the highest. Each re-predicts 64 of the examples with that example
        hidden from itself. The pass that explains them better gets more say in the final answer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>One bit step</h2>
  <p>
    This repeats 8 times per pass. During training the question's earlier bits are the true ones; when answering, they
    are its own predictions. Weights never change when answering.
  </p>
  <div class="card">
    <StepDiagram />
  </div>

  <h2>Inside real predictions</h2>
  <p>
    These are recordings from the trained model on fresh episodes. Pick a relation and step through the bits. On
    <code>add</code>, low-bit-first, the attention gathers in the shaded intersection — examples whose bit
    <code>k</code> of <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> match the question — and, within it, only those with the same carry.
    Try <code>min</code> or <code>a*b</code> to see it fail.
  </p>
  <Inspector />

  <h2>Which direction does it trust?</h2>
  <p>
    Average weight given to the high-bit-first pass over 16 episodes per relation. It picks low-first for carries,
    high-first for <code>max</code>, and doesn't care for bitwise relations. On the held-out <code>min</code> it
    hesitates — which is exactly where it fails. <span class="muted">* never seen in training.</span>
  </p>
  <div class="card">
    <TrustStrip rows={trustRows} />
  </div>

  <h2>Size</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Parameters</td><td class="num">{thousands(model.parameters)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Memory (GRU hidden size)</td><td class="num">{model.config.hidden}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Attention heads</td><td class="num">{model.config.heads}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Self-check examples per pass</td><td class="num">{model.config.probes}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Examples per question</td><td class="num">999 (fixed)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bit width trained / supported</td><td class="num">8 / up to {model.config.max_width}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">Source: <code>src/teenie/bits/model.py</code>.</p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .ideas {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }


  .ideas p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  table {
    max-width: 520px;
  }
</style>
